  slug="cut-vacancy-days-between-tenants"
  eyebrow="Turnovers"
  title="How to Cut Vacancy Days Between Tenants: A Turnover Timeline That Actually Works"
  description="Most rental vacancy is scheduling, not work. The pre-move-out walkthrough, trade sequencing, and timeline that compress a unit turn by a week or more."
>
<p>Most vacancy days are not caused by the work. They are caused by the waiting between the work: waiting to see the unit, waiting for quotes, waiting for trades to fit you in, waiting for the clean, waiting for photos. The single biggest lever for cutting vacancy is moving every decision that can happen before move-out to before move-out, so the day the keys come back, work starts instead of scheduling. Landlords who run turns this way routinely save a week or more per turn, and every one of those days is a day of rent recovered. Here is the timeline that does it.</p><h2>Why the standard turnover timeline bleeds days</h2><p>The default sequence most landlords run looks like this. Tenant leaves. A few days later someone walks the unit and makes a list. The list goes out for quotes, which takes days more. Work gets approved, then waits for an opening in a contractor's schedule. The paint happens, then a gap, then flooring, then a gap, then the clean. Photos happen when someone gets around to it, and only then does marketing start. None of these steps is slow by itself. Stacked in series, they turn a few days of actual work into weeks of vacancy, and the unit earns nothing the entire time. We put real numbers on what each of those days costs in <a href='/articles/cost-of-a-vacant-rental-day/'>what a vacant day actually costs a Middle Tennessee landlord</a>, and the short version is that the schedule, not the scope, is usually the expensive part.</p><h2>The compressed timeline</h2><h3>Before move-out: scope, quote, and schedule</h3><ul><li><strong>Walk the unit two to three weeks before the tenant leaves.</strong> Tennessee landlords can access an occupied unit with proper notice, and a cooperative tenant plus a scheduled visit gets you eyes on the unit while there is still time to plan. You will not see everything with furniture in place, but you will see eighty percent: paint condition, flooring wear, appliance state, caulk and grout, and anything the tenant mentions on the way out.</li><li><strong>Build the scope and get the quote before the unit is empty.</strong> This is the single highest-value move in the whole process. A quote in hand before move-out means approval happens before move-out, which means the crew is scheduled before move-out. This is exactly how we run turns, a flat quote before the unit is vacant, so the number is approved and the calendar is booked while rent is still coming in.</li><li><strong>Order the long-lead items now.</strong> Flooring material, appliances, and special-order fixtures have lead times. Ordering after move-out puts their lead time inside your vacancy. Ordering before puts it inside the tenant's last weeks instead.</li><li><strong>Start marketing before the unit is empty.</strong> If the current condition allows it, list with existing photos or schedule the photo day for right after completion, and start collecting interest while the work happens.</li></ul><h3>Move-out day: inspect and release</h3><p>Do the move-out inspection the day the keys come back, against the same checklist the scope was built from. Two things happen here: the deposit assessment gets documented while the evidence is fresh, and the work order gets released with any adjustments the empty unit reveals. The difference between wear and tear and chargeable damage matters for that deposit paperwork, and we covered where Tennessee draws the line in <a href='/articles/wear-and-tear-vs-damage-tennessee/'>wear and tear versus damage</a>.</p><h3>The work week: sequence the trades so nobody waits</h3><p>Trade order is its own day-saver, because the wrong order forces rework and return trips. The sequence that works:</p><ol><li><strong>Repairs and anything that opens walls or floors first.</strong> Plumbing, electrical, drywall patches, door and hardware repairs.</li><li><strong>Paint second,</strong> after the drywall dust and before the new flooring goes down, so overspray and drips land on the old floor.</li><li><strong>Flooring third,</strong> onto finished painted rooms.</li><li><strong>Detail work fourth.</strong> Fixtures, switch plates, caulk lines, bulbs, smoke detector batteries, filters.</li><li><strong>The deep clean last,</strong> once every trade is out, so it happens exactly once.</li><li><strong>Photos the same day as the clean,</strong> not next week. The unit never looks better than this day.</li></ol><p>When one crew self-performs multiple trades, the gaps between steps collapse further, which is most of the argument for turning the whole unit with one call instead of coordinating four contractors who have never met. Our <a href='/turnovers/'>turnover packages</a> are built around exactly this sequencing.</p><h3>After the work: show immediately</h3><p>Applications, screening, and lease signing take days by themselves. Running them in parallel with the final work days, using scheduled showings the day after the clean, means the new lease starts days sooner. The goal state is a signed lease with a start date inside the same week the unit finishes, which only happens when marketing started before the paint did.</p><h2>Where turns lose days anyway, and how to buffer them</h2><p>Even a well-planned turn has failure modes, and the fix for each one is cheap when it is set up in advance:</p><ul><li><strong>The furniture reveal.</strong> Some damage only shows once the unit is empty, which is why the pre-move-out quote should carry a small allowance for surprises and why the move-out inspection happens day one, not day four. A crew already scheduled can absorb a modest scope addition. A crew not yet called cannot.</li><li><strong>Utilities cut off between tenants.</strong> No power means no paint, no clean, and no showings. Keep utilities transferring to the owner's name automatically between tenants rather than letting them lapse, and confirm it the week before move-out.</li><li><strong>Key and access friction.</strong> A tenant who mails keys late or a unit with no lockbox can idle a scheduled crew. Put key return in the move-out instructions, and get a lockbox on the door the day the unit empties.</li><li><strong>The holdover.</strong> A tenant who does not actually leave collapses the whole schedule. Confirm the move-out date in writing in the final month and have showings and crew starts contingent on confirmed possession, not the lease date alone.</li></ul><h2>Standardize the unit, compress every future turn</h2><p>The second lever is bigger than it looks: make units identical wherever you can. One paint color across the portfolio means touch-up paint always matches and full repaints happen less often. One flooring product means leftover planks from any job can fix damage in any unit, without a discontinued-product hunt three years later. Consistent fixtures and hardware mean parts on the truck fit every door. Standardization converts future turns from custom projects into repeatable ones, and repeatable is fast. While you are standardizing, it is also the moment to ask which upgrades are worth folding in, because a turn is the cheapest time to do them. We ranked those in <a href='/articles/what-upgrades-actually-raise-rent-middle-tennessee/'>which turnover upgrades actually raise rent</a>.</p><h2>The one-week turn is a scheduling achievement</h2><p>A typical refresh-level turn is only a few days of actual work. Getting the calendar to match the work is the whole game: scope early, quote before vacancy, order materials ahead, sequence the trades, clean once, photograph immediately, and lease in parallel. If you would rather hand that whole sequence to one crew, that is what we do all week across Middle Tennessee. Send the unit through our <a href='/work-order/'>service request form</a> before your tenant moves out, and you will have a flat quote and a scheduled start before the keys come back.</p>
